NOTES AND QUESTIONS ON GUNN V. MINTON
The Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act, 21 U.S.C. § 302 et seq. (FDCA), regulates (among other things) the labeling of pharmaceuticals but creates no private right of action for violations of the statute. Rather, the federal government itself enforces the FDCA. In Merrell Dow Pharmaceuticals Inc. v. Thompson, 478 U.S. 804 (1986), the plaintiffs brought a number of state-law causes of action in Ohio state court against Merrell Dow Pharmaceuticals for injuries related to their use of the drug Bendectin. One of the claims in the complaint alleged that Bendectin was “misbranded” in violation of the FDCA, and that this violation of the FDCA “constitutes a rebuttable presumption of negligence.” Thus, although the plaintiff alleged a claim for negligence under the Ohio common law of tort, this claim involved a question of whether the defendant violated a federal law. (Recall that there could be no separate claim under the FDCA, because there is no private right of action under that statute.) Does such a claim “arise under” federal law? The Supreme Court answered that question in the negative. Today, with the benefit of Gunn v. Minton, would the result be the same? Why or why not?

The court in Dillon v. Medtronic faced a similar question under a related statute, the Medical Device Amendments to the FDCA. The analysis in Dillon, though, relied on the fact that the allegation of negligence based on a violation of federal law was just one of many theories raised in the case. What if it had been the only theory of liability?
